A||N||3||bilbo||SWITCHING FRONT FASCIAS!!||||9||
Z||000000||bilbo||12-12-2000||07:30 PM||||WHEN YOU SAY THE MOLDINGS WON'T LINE UP WHEN SWITCHING FRONT FASCIAS FROM A GT TO A COUPE WHAT DO YOU MEAN AND CAN IT BE MADE TOO FIT AT ALL!!?? THANKS||||reg||9
Z||000001||fropuff||12-12-2000||08:56 PM||||As far as I know, the fascias line up correctly.  I think they meant that the small triangular mouldings will not be the same.  On some models, there are grooves in the molding; on others, they are smooth.  Just try to find a fascia with a molding that matches.  At least this is what I think; I'm not completely sure.  Sorry, that's all I've got.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||12-12-2000||10:04 PM||||The moldings might also be talking about is the plastic "egg crate" that absorbs the shock before it gets to the metal bumper. When I changed the GT nose to a Formula nose on my 85 GT I had to change it. I fished around and found one at FOCOA that came from a Formula, this way I still have protection. I also went to the my old 87 coupe and took what wasn't bent or destroyed from it. Also don't forget about the wheel wells! You might be able to cut them to fit but coupe wheel wells are everywhere. GT wheelwells are the hard ones to find, if they are in good shape maybe you could find someone who needs them more than you!<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 12-12-2000).]||||reg||6
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||12-12-2000||10:12 PM||||You can get by without the impact web under the fascia. The moulding being referred to is the body line stuff. The '85 GT's and '85 &'86 ('87's?) SE have grooved body mouldings. The '86 thru '88 GT's have smooth mouldings.||||reg||1
